const EN = `# Where to Stay in Vietnam

Finding a good place to sleep in Hanoi and Ho Chi Minh City (HCMC) is easy. Rooms are good value and there are many choices. This guide helps you pick the right area and avoid common problems.

---

## 1. Types of Places to Stay

- **Hostels:** A bed in a shared dorm. Cheapest option, and good for meeting other travelers.
- **Mini-hotels and budget hotels:** Small, family-run hotels with private rooms. Very common in both cities.
- **Mid-range hotels:** Clean, comfortable, often with breakfast, a lift and a front desk open all day.
- **Luxury hotels:** International brands and fine hotels with pools, spas and city views.
- **Serviced apartments:** A room with a kitchen and washing machine. Good for stays of a week or more.
- **Homestays and guesthouses:** Rooms in a local home. Simple, friendly and a good way to meet local people.

---

## 2. Rough Prices (US Dollars per Night)

- **Hostel dorm bed:** about $5–12
- **Budget private room:** about $15–35
- **Mid-range hotel:** about $40–90
- **Luxury hotel:** about $120 and up

These are rough guides only. Prices go up in busy seasons and on weekends. Always check the final price on the booking page before you pay.

---

## 3. Best Areas in Hanoi

- **Old Quarter:** The busiest area. Many hotels, street food and shops, and you can walk to most sights. It is noisy until late at night.
- **French Quarter:** Wide streets, the Opera House and quieter hotels. Good for comfort and a calmer stay.
- **Ba Dinh:** Near the Ho Chi Minh Mausoleum and many historic sights. Quiet and green.
- **West Lake (Tay Ho):** A relaxed area with cafes and lake views. It is far from the Old Quarter, so you will need a taxi or ride-hailing app.

Hanoi airport is about 45 minutes or more from the center by road. Plan your arrival time with this in mind.

---

## 4. Best Areas in Ho Chi Minh City

- **District 1:** The center of the city. Near Ben Thanh Market, the main sights and many restaurants. Best choice for a first visit.
- **Pham Ngu Lao and Bui Vien:** The backpacker area in District 1. Cheap rooms and lively nightlife. Very loud at night.
- **District 3:** Quieter and more local. Good cafes and food, and it is close to District 1.
- **Thao Dien:** A calm, leafy area with cafes and international restaurants. It is across the river, so you will need a taxi or ride-hailing app.

HCMC airport is close to the center. The trip is usually about 30 minutes, but traffic can make it longer.

---

## 5. When to Book

- Book early for **Tet (Lunar New Year)**, usually in late January or February. Prices rise and many shops and restaurants close.
- Other busy times are **30 April – 1 May**, **2 September** (National Day) and the year-end holidays.
- Outside these times, you can often book a few days before arrival.
- For the best rooms in the Old Quarter or near the center, book at least a few weeks ahead.

---

## 6. How to Book

- Use a trusted booking website or app and compare a few options.
- Read recent reviews, not only the score. Look at reviews from the last few months.
- Check the free cancellation rules. Plans can change.
- Make sure the price shows the number of guests, the number of nights and all taxes.
- Save the booking confirmation and the hotel address on your phone.

---

## 7. Check-in and Your Passport

- Normal check-in is around 2:00 pm and check-out around 12:00 pm. Ask the hotel if you need an early check-in or a late check-out.
- Hotels in Vietnam must register foreign guests. You will need to show your passport at check-in.
- Some hotels keep your passport for a short time. Ask for it back the same day.
- Take a photo of your passport page and your visa before you travel.

---

## 8. Payment and Deposits

- Many hotels ask for a deposit at check-in. This is normal. Ask how much it is and when you get it back.
- Small hotels and hostels may prefer cash. Larger hotels accept cards.
- Cards can have extra fees. Ask before you pay.
- Always take a receipt for deposits and payments.

---

## 9. What to Check Before You Book

- **Location:** Look at the map. Is it close to the places you want to visit?
- **Window:** Some budget rooms have no window. Check the room photos and the description.
- **Lift:** Many small hotels are tall and narrow with many stairs. Ask if there is a lift if you have heavy bags.
- **Air conditioning:** Very important in the hot months.
- **Heating:** Hanoi can be cold and damp from December to February. Many budget rooms have no heating.
- **Breakfast and Wi-Fi:** Check if they are included.

---

## 10. Noise, Safety and Comfort

- Motorbikes and street noise are common in both cities. If you sleep lightly, ask for a room at the back or on a higher floor.
- Use the room safe or the hotel front desk for your passport and valuables.
- Lock your door and your window when you go out.
- Ask the front desk to help with taxi or ride-hailing pick-ups. This is useful late at night.

---

## 11. Common Problems and How to Avoid Them

- **Hotels with similar names:** In Hanoi's Old Quarter, some hotels copy the names of popular hotels. Check the exact address on your booking before you go in.
- **Taxi drivers who say your hotel is closed or full:** This is a common trick to take you to another hotel. Do not believe it. Call the hotel to check.
- **Rooms that do not match the photos:** Read recent reviews and look at traveler photos.
- **Extra charges:** Ask about all fees before you pay, such as for laundry, minibar and early check-in.
- **Room price changes:** Keep your booking confirmation. Show it if the price is different at check-in.

---

## 12. Practical Tips

- Electricity in Vietnam is 220 V. Sockets are usually Type A, C or F, so bring an adapter if you need one.
- Tipping is not required in hotels, but small tips for good service are welcome.
- Carry a card with the hotel name and address in Vietnamese. It helps taxi drivers find it.
- Hotel staff often speak some English. They can help with tours, transport and restaurant tips.
- If your plans change, tell the hotel early. Many will help you.`

